<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Third Portuguese Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1974 CE – present · capital Lisbon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's democratic republic, born in the Carnation Revolution of 1974, which ended the empire and took the country into Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 25 April 1974 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armed-forces-movement">Armed Forces Movement</a>, junior officers weary of the colonial wars, overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo (Portugal)</a> in the almost bloodless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnation-revolution">Carnation Revolution</a>. A turbulent revolutionary period followed, with nationalisations and land seizures, until a moderate coup on 25 November 1975.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Constitution of 1976 established a semi-presidential democracy. Portugal granted independence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-mozambique">Mozambique</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-tome">São Tomé</a> in 1974-1975, receiving hundreds of thousands of returnees. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mario-soares">Mário Soares's</a> Socialists and the Social Democrats alternated in power. Portugal joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> in 1986 and adopted the euro in 1999, and handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the same year.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Portugal remains a parliamentary democracy and member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>. After the 2011 debt crisis and bailout, it recovered with growing tourism and investment.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/third-portuguese-republic.jpg" alt="Third Portuguese Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Columbano Bordalo Pinheiro Vector: Vítor Luís Rodrigues, António Martins-Tuválkin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(official).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1974 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lisbon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>António Ramalho Eanes, Mário Soares, Aníbal Cavaco Silva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Portuguese Republic, Portugal</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=third-portuguese-republic" aria-label="Third Portuguese Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4453 789 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Third Portuguese Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4773 1045L4773 1045M4773 1045L4773 1044"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1045h0M4773 1044h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lisbon. <a href="/atlas?year=2000&amp;focus=third-portuguese-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
